{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T1-001", "approach": "LEX5B", "approach_base": "LEX5B", "flags": {}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "corpus": "K", "corpus_set": "BOOK_en", "answer_text": "Faraday explains that the cup forms because the heat of the flame creates an ascending current of air that cools the candle's exterior, while the interior melts.\n\n- As air comes to the candle it moves upwards by the force of the current the heat produces, cooling all the sides of the wax or tallow so the edge stays much cooler than the part within (passages 3 and 5).\n- The part within melts by the flame running down the wick as far as it can before being extinguished, but the outside does not melt (passages 3 and 5).\n- The cup is thus formed by this regular ascending current playing on all sides, keeping the exterior cool (passage 3).\n- Gravity keeps the fluid horizontal, so if the cup is not horizontal the fluid runs away in guttering (passage 3).\n- The only reason the candle does not burn all down the side of the wick is that the melted tallow extinguishes the flame (passage 1).\n- No fuel would serve for a candle that cannot form this cup, except Irish bogwood, which holds its own fuel like a sponge (passage 3).", "abstained": false, "retrieved_units": [{"text": "[stripped: 774 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o029", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b52", "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b53"], "kind": "package", "score": null, "rank": null, "span": [21708, 22482], "meta": {"chunk": 2563, "completed": 6, "prefix": 0, "provenance": [["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", 21708, 22482, "body"]]}}, {"text": "[stripped: 1009 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday